---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import { highlight } from '../lib/highlight'
import { BUCKET, MB, mp3Facts, pcmBytes, peaksBytes } from '../lib/facts'
import { MUSIC, MUSIC_TITLE } from '../music'
import { HowStencil } from '../islands/HowStencil'
import { HowFrame } from '../islands/HowFrame'
import { WS_STYLE, SILLON_STYLE, WS_REACT, SILLON_REACT } from '../why-snippets'
import { col, meta, chapterN, lede, ledeTitle, ledeText, pageEnd, section } from '../cls'

const int = (x: number) => Math.round(x).toLocaleString('en-US')
const size = (bytes: number) => (bytes >= 10 * MB ? `${Math.round(bytes / MB)} MB` : bytes >= MB ? `${(bytes / MB).toFixed(1)} MB` : `${Math.round(bytes / 1024)} kB`)

// What each library keeps once this site's song is loaded, by arithmetic.
const song = mp3Facts(MUSIC)
const squares = [
  { label: 'The MP3 itself', bytes: song.bytes },
  { label: 'What wavesurfer.js keeps: the audio, decoded at 8 kHz', bytes: pcmBytes(song.duration, 8000, song.channels) },
  { label: 'What sillon keeps: its peaks', bytes: peaksBytes(song.samples).bytes },
]
const biggest = Math.max(...squares.map((s) => s.bytes))

// Sections after the first sit below a rule. The rail's own rule divides the first from the hero.
const docP = 'mt-6 max-w-text text-ink-2'
// Two approaches, one above the other.
const versus = 'mt-10 grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)] gap-5 [&_pre]:text-[13px]'
const versusWho = `${meta} mb-2 block`
const tradeoff = 'border-t border-rule py-3.5 text-ink-2 first:border-t-0 first:pt-0'
---
<Page title="Why sillon" description="Why we built a waveform library when wavesurfer.js exists, and where wavesurfer.js is still the better choice." page="why">
  <script is:inline slot="head">
    // Counts React commits per root, so the playback demo can show how often its waveform renders.
    // Installed before React loads; if React DevTools is already here, its hook is wrapped instead.
    (() => {
      const counts = (window.__commits = new Map())
      const count = (root) => counts.set(root.containerInfo, (counts.get(root.containerInfo) ?? 0) + 1)
      const hook = window.__REACT_DEVTOOLS_GLOBAL_HOOK__
      if (hook) {
        const prev = hook.onCommitFiberRoot
        hook.onCommitFiberRoot = function (id, root, ...rest) { count(root); return prev?.call(this, id, root, ...rest) }
      } else {
        window.__REACT_DEVTOOLS_GLOBAL_HOOK__ = {
          supportsFiber: true, renderers: new Map(), inject: () => 1, checkDCE() {},
          onCommitFiberRoot: (id, root) => count(root), onCommitFiberUnmount() {}, onPostCommitFiberRoot() {}, setStrictMode() {},
        }
      }
    })()
  </script>

  <section class={`${col} pt-[72px] narrow:pt-12`} aria-labelledby="doc-title">
    <h1 id="doc-title" class="text-[40px]/[1.12] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-balance narrow:text-[32px]">Why sillon</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-text text-[19px]/[1.55] text-ink-2 narrow:text-[17px]">
      wavesurfer.js is the standard way to show audio on the web, and it's good at it. We built sillon because of what we
      kept running into when using it in React apps: styling it, putting our own components on it, and what it holds on to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <main class={`${col} ${pageEnd} [&_code]:text-ink`}>
    <section id="styling" class="mt-[72px] scroll-mt-[76px]">
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>01</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Styling it with CSS</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>wavesurfer.js paints its bars onto a canvas, so their look comes from options you pass in JavaScript. In sillon the bars are a stencil, and whatever sits behind them shows through. The look is plain CSS.</p>
      </div>
      <HowStencil client:only="react"><div slot="fallback" class="h-[140px]" aria-hidden="true"></div></HowStencil>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>wavesurfer.js</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(WS_STYLE, 'javascript')} /></div>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_STYLE, 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="react" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>02</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Your components on the waveform</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}><code>@wavesurfer/react</code> wraps wavesurfer.js in a hook, but the waveform still lives outside React: markers and regions come from plugins, which build their own elements. In sillon every part is a component, so a comment pin is the avatar component you already have.</p>
      </div>
      <div class={versus}>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>wavesurfer.js with @wavesurfer/react</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(WS_REACT, 'tsx')} /></div>
        <div><span class={versusWho}>sillon</span><div data-code set:html={highlight(SILLON_REACT, 'tsx')} /></div>
      </div>
      <p class={docP}>Being components doesn't mean re-rendering. While audio plays, React doesn't render at all: each frame, sillon moves the played part and the playhead with a CSS transform. Press play and watch.</p>
      <HowFrame client:only="react"><div slot="fallback" class="h-[540px]" aria-hidden="true"></div></HowFrame>
    </section>

    <section id="peaks" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>03</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>It keeps peaks, not audio</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>
          wavesurfer.js keeps the decoded audio and draws from it. sillon keeps only what it draws: the loudest sample in every {BUCKET},
          plus coarser copies for zooming out, and lets the audio go. Here is what each holds for this site's {Math.round(song.duration / 60)}-minute song, drawn to scale.
        </p>
      </div>
      {/* Squares whose areas are byte counts. */}
      <figure class="[--big:300px] narrow:[--big:160px]">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(4,auto)] justify-start gap-x-8 gap-y-3.5 narrow:grid-cols-[auto_minmax(0,1fr)] narrow:gap-x-4 narrow:gap-y-5">
          {squares.map((s) => (
            <div class="group contents">
              <div class="row-1 aspect-square w-[max(3px,calc(var(--side)*var(--big)))] self-end rounded-[3px] bg-rival group-last:bg-accent narrow:row-auto narrow:self-start narrow:justify-self-end" style={`--side: ${Math.sqrt(s.bytes / biggest).toFixed(4)}`}></div>
              <div class="row-2 grid min-w-[120px] max-w-[160px] content-start gap-0.5 narrow:row-auto">
                <b class="text-[20px] font-medium tabular-nums">{size(s.bytes)}</b>
                <span class="text-[14px]/[1.4] text-ink-2">{s.label}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <figcaption class={`${meta} mt-6 max-w-text`}>
          Area is bytes. {MUSIC_TITLE}, {int(song.duration)} s, {song.channels} channels. Decoded audio is seconds × samples per second × channels × 4 bytes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class={docP}>A page with many waveforms on it, like a feed of tracks or a chat full of voice notes, holds peaks for each of them instead of audio. Zooming reads a few stored values per bar instead of going back to the samples.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="tradeoffs" class={section}>
      <div class={lede}>
        <span class={chapterN}>04</span>
        <h2 class={ledeTitle}>Where wavesurfer.js is better</h2>
        <p class={ledeText}>If none of the above gets in your way, wavesurfer.js is the safer choice.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="mb-10 grid max-w-text [&_b]:font-semibold [&_b]:text-ink">
        <li class={tradeoff}><b>It does more.</b> Plugins for spectrograms, recording, a timeline, a minimap and volume envelopes. sillon has none of these yet.</li>
        <li class={tradeoff}><b>It works anywhere.</b> No framework needed. sillon is React only.</li>
        <li class={tradeoff}><b>It has been around.</b> Years of use in production, and a large community. sillon is new.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</Page>
